# Task 12 – Re-evaluate and Adapt the Design

## A. Impact Analysis
The university introduces e-bikes, delivery vans and one-day passes.
The parking facility also expands to five floors with different
numbers of slots.

The Vehicle hierarchy, pricing rules and pass management must be
updated. A jagged array supports different slot counts on each floor.

## B. Evaluation of the Original Design
Inheritance allows new vehicle categories to be represented by
subclasses. However, pricing rules should be separated from vehicle
classes to make future changes easier.

## C. Adaptation or Redesign
The revised design uses:
- Vehicle: Abstract superclass.
- Car: Represents cars.
- EBike: Represents e-bikes with a flat fee.
- DeliveryVan: Represents delivery vans.
- Pass: Interface for parking passes.
- OneDayPass: Represents a one-day pass.

The example uses separate subclasses and a Pass interface.

## D. Evidence-Based Defence
The program demonstrates the three vehicle categories and a
one-day pass. Each vehicle type provides its own fee calculation.

## Complexity Analysis
- Fee calculation: O(1).
- Pass validation: O(1).
- Space per vehicle: O(1), excluding object overhead.

## Trade-offs
Inheritance makes vehicle-specific behaviour clear. Separating
pricing into a PricingRule abstraction would make it easier to
change time-based pricing without modifying vehicle classes.

## Conclusion
The design should be modified to support new vehicle categories,
one-day passes and different numbers of slots per floor.